From 4aaf036f27cd0d69eea042cb9d76eb02b8100016 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 13:27:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Engagement redesign: retrieval-first learning, spaced review,
 Defend the Faith
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Research (active recall/spaced repetition literature, Duolingo's own
documented mechanics) pointed to three concrete gaps in the previous
Classroom loop:

1. Retrieval was backwards — verses showed before the learner answered, so
   responding was really just re-reading. ClassroomPage now hides scripture
   evidence until after the first attempt for each stage (attempt from
   memory, then reveal + correct), matching how retrieval practice actually
   strengthens memory versus read-then-answer.

2. No spaced repetition existed. standardsProgress.js adds a real SM-2-lite
   schedule (scheduleNextReview/getDueForReview, 1/3/7/21/60-day intervals
   that grow on success and reset short on failure) as a separate store
   from the existing level map, so nothing that already reads plain levels
   (Map lock logic, isSubjectMastered) had to change.

3. No engagement loop existed. New Edge Function defend-the-faith turns
   spaced review into something worth returning for: every standard's own
   Defense-level evidence-of-learning already names a real opposing
   objection (e.g. the trinitarian claim about echad/Genesis 2:24) —
   Jeremiah now argues that exact position and the learner has to hold the
   doctrine in a live multi-turn exchange, not a flashcard. Outcome drives
   the review schedule directly. New streak module (core/streak/streak.js)
   only increments on a genuine graded action (a real Classroom grading
   result or a completed Defend round), deliberately avoiding Duolingo's
   documented failure mode of streak-protecting speed-runs that skip real
   learning. Home now surfaces a Review Queue (standards due today) and a
   loss-aversion-framed streak pill.

